  "query_preview": "..\n\nBy Wednesday, a deep upper low will be moving into northern CA \nwhile the high moves a bit closer to the MS valley. This will \ncontinue to squeeze subtropical moisture into NM, from a more due \nsouth direction through Thursday as the low moves farther inland. \nDeterministic and probabilistic guidance continue to paint \nwidespread QPF over NM Wednesday and Thursday with PWATs \ncontinuing to hover within a 1.0 to 1.3 inch range. Storm motions \nwould likely increase to a 15 to 25 mph pace in the confluent \nsoutherly steering flow both of these days, but the day-by-day \nblotting of precipitation over northern and central NM will start \nto lead to wetter soils and any training would pose runoff and \nflooding concerns, mostly on small, localized scales. There are \nalso large uncertainties with excessive mid to high cloud cover \nwhich may impede destabilization during the afternoons. \nTemperatures will run considerably cooler (about 3 to 6 degrees \nbelow normal), especially in western and central zones, and \nprolonged cloud cover could even push these values lower.\n\nBy Friday the upper low will move farther inland with a trailing \ntrough over the Great Basin. This will introduce a drier and more \nwesterly wind component aloft into the northwestern third of NM, \nreducing rain chances there, but remaining areas should continue \nto observe scattered to numerous showers and storms. Westerlies \novertake more of the forecast area into Saturday, reducing storm \nchances more. There are some discrepancies among deterministic \nmodel members by Sunday, but the consensus is that the driest air \nwill be found in western areas of the forecast area with an \noverall continued decrease in storm coverage for all zones. The \nexception to this generalization might be the northeastern corner \nof NM where another weak, but moist backdoor front could enter.",
